{0 Fetch}

Use {!Workero.fetch} inside a handler to call another server. It waits for
response headers; read the response body separately.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let upstream =
    fetch ~method_:POST
      ~headers:[ ("content-type", "application/json") ]
      ~body:{|{"ping":true}|} "https://example.com/api"
  in
  match Response.status upstream with
  | 200 -> Response.text (Response.body_text upstream)
  | status ->
      Response.text ~status:`Bad_Gateway
        (Printf.sprintf "upstream returned %d\n" status)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

The default method is [GET]. A [~body] is raw bytes and does not set
[content-type]. Include that header when the server expects one. A body with
[GET] or [HEAD] raises [Invalid_argument]. A failed request raises
{!Workero.Js_error}; catch it if your handler must return a response on a
network failure.

Call [fetch] inside a handler or a function passed to
{!Workero.Context.wait_until}. To forward the response without reading its
body, use {!Workero.Response.copy}. If you set [~headers] on the copy, include
the existing {!Workero.Response.headers}: [~headers] replaces them. See
{{!page-requests}Requests and responses} for an example and
{{!page-streams}Streams} for passing through a large body.

To call another Worker through a service binding or access static assets,
use {{!page-fetcher}Fetcher}. See {!Workero.fetch} for the complete interface.
